# "PDS hostname move and public URL scheme" - Stage 1 of 2 (hostname move
# only). Wildcard handle hosts still plain-proxy every path, exactly as
# they did before this move - Stage 2 switches them to the path-based
# profile redirect, deployed separately and only once this stage is live
# and verified.
#
# INCIDENT, 2026-09-14 through 2026-09-17: the first version of this stage
# removed `tls { on_demand }` from every explicit host block (pds./app./
# www./sunnahsky.com), giving them "regular" managed automatic HTTPS
# instead - a TLS provisioning mode this deployment had never used before
# (the pre-move Caddyfile put `tls { on_demand }` on literally every
# block, apex included). That broke TLS entirely for app.sunnahsky.com
# (previously live, working, serving real traffic) and pds.sunnahsky.com
# for three days - `openssl s_client` showed "no peer certificate
# available" / TLS alert 80 (internal_error), meaning Caddy's regular-
# managed cert path never actually served the certificate at handshake
# time for these names, despite a valid cert existing on disk for both
# and /tls-check correctly approving both on request. Root cause not
# fully isolated (likely an interaction between the global on_demand_tls
# block and mixing an exact-host "regular" automation policy with an
# overlapping wildcard on-demand policy for the same subject names -
# every one of the three broken hosts is a single-label subdomain that
# textually matches `*.sunnahsky.com`; the apex, which matches no
# wildcard, was the only one of the four names in that policy that kept
# working). Mitigated by reverting every explicit host block to
# `tls { on_demand }`, matching the one TLS provisioning mode this setup
# has ever actually run reliably. Do not remove `tls { on_demand }` from
# any block below without a real test that survives a `docker restart
# caddy` and confirms the exact host actually serves TLS afterward - a
# green `caddy validate` and a same-second curl are not sufficient
# verification for this class of bug.
{
	email abdullahalam@salafibookstore.com
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://localhost:3000/tls-check
	}
}

pds.sunnahsky.com {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	# /tls-check is the on_demand_tls "ask" endpoint above. Caddy calls it
	# directly on localhost:3000, never through this site block, so nothing
	# legitimate reaches it via the public host - and with a per-hostname
	# approval limiter behind it, a public caller could otherwise spend a
	# real handle's budget on purpose. Answered here, before reverse_proxy
	# (Caddy orders `respond` ahead of `reverse_proxy` regardless of
	# position in the block, and `respond` is terminal). The PDS also
	# refuses the route when X-Forwarded-For is present, as a backstop.
	@tlscheck path /tls-check
	respond @tlscheck 404
	reverse_proxy http://localhost:3000
}

sunnahsky.com {
	# Deliberately NOT on_demand: PDS_HOSTNAME moved away from this domain
	# and it was never added to ADDITIONAL_APPROVED_DOMAINS in
	# services/pds/index.ts, so /tls-check now rejects it outright
	# ("handles are not provided on this domain") - putting it on_demand
	# broke it fresh on the very next `docker restart caddy`, even though
	# a valid, already-issued certificate for it already existed on disk.
	# Regular managed TLS works correctly here (this is the one host of
	# the four broken-then-half-fixed above that has zero wildcard
	# overlap with *.sunnahsky.com, and it never actually broke under
	# regular mode - only pds./app./www. did). Caddy renews this on its
	# own background schedule without ever calling the ask endpoint.
	root * /etc/caddy/sunnahsky-web
	try_files {path} /index.html
	file_server
}

app.sunnahsky.com {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	redir https://sunnahsky.com{uri} 301
}

# NOT YET WORKING even under on_demand: /tls-check rejects this domain
# outright (`NotFound: handle not found for this domain`) since
# services/pds/index.ts's ADDITIONAL_APPROVED_DOMAINS only ever listed
# `app.sunnahsky.com` - www.sunnahsky.com was never added when this stage
# was built. Needs a PDS code fix + image rebuild before this block can
# actually issue a certificate; the block stays here, consistent with the
# rest of the config, so it starts working the moment that ships without
# a further Caddy change.
www.sunnahsky.com {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	redir https://sunnahsky.com{uri} 301
}

# Stage 1 only: every handle host still proxies straight through, same as
# before the move - no path-based redirect yet (that's Stage 2).
*.guest.sunnahsky.com, *.sunnahsky.com {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy http://localhost:3000
}
